        "# First frame + last frame\n\nTwo keyframes: the video starts at one image and lands on the other.\nDistilled LoRA, 8 steps, cfg 1.0.\n\n**Both `LoadImage` nodes point at ComfyUI's stock `example.png`** so the graph\nruns on a fresh install. Point the second one at a different image -- that is the\nwhole feature.\n\n## This one works differently from the other I2V graphs\n\n`LTXVImgToVideoConditionOnly` (used by the other three) writes into the latent\nand nothing else. **`LTXVAddGuideAdvanced` rewrites the conditioning too**, so\nthe two guides chain through `positive`/`negative`/`latent` and the `CFGGuider`\nreads from the *second* guide, not from `LTXVConditioning`. If you add a third\nkeyframe, extend the chain the same way.\n\nIt also does its own resizing (lanczos), `LTXVPreprocess` and optional blur\ninternally (`guide.py:116-133`), which is why there is no `ImageScale` +\n`LTXVPreprocess` pair in front of it.\n\n## The knobs\n\n| Widget | Note |\n|---|---|\n| `frame_idx` | `0` is the first frame, `-1` the last. For 9+ frame videos anything else is **rounded down to a multiple of 8**. |\n| `strength` | `1.0` locks the keyframe. Lower it if the two ends are far apart and the motion between them looks like a crossfade. |\n| `crf` | `29` (the node default). Looser than the `18` the plain I2V graphs use -- a frame the model must *arrive at* wants more slack than one it starts from. |\n| `blur_radius` | Raise it to buy motion at the cost of keyframe fidelity. |\n\n## Two things to know before you lower `strength`\n\n1. **`strength != 1.0` turns SageAttention off for this graph.** A guide below\n   full strength takes LTX's guide-mask attention path, which passes\n   `low_precision_attention=False` on every sub-call, so `attention_sage`\n   correctly demotes to SDPA. The `LTXV Attention Backend` node stays wired up\n   and simply stops paying off. That is the documented behaviour, not a bug.\n2. **Keyframes cost tokens.** Each guide appends its frame to the attended\n   sequence, so peak VRAM and step time both rise slightly with keyframe count.\n\nEverything about the loaders and the audio branch is below.\n\n## Why this graph looks like it does\n\nTwo things in the earlier version of these workflows could never have run, and\nboth were found by actually executing them:\n\n**1. The text encoder.** `ltx-2-3-22b-text_encoder.safetensors` is *not* a text\nencoder -- it holds four tensors, `text_embedding_projection.*`. LTX-2.3's text\nencoder is **Gemma-3-12B**, and the projection aggregates its 49 hidden states\n(49 x 3840 = 188160). `comfy/sd.py` only takes the LTX-2.3 Gemma branch\n(`ltxav_te` + `LTXAVGemmaTokenizer`, sd.py:1809) when handed **exactly two**\nstate dicts. A single-file `CLIPLoader(type=ltxv)` falls into the LTX-0.9 **T5**\nbranch instead and dies with `Cannot copy out of meta tensor`.\n\nSo: **`DualCLIPLoader`, type `ltxv`**, Gemma first (sd.py:1812 reads the\ntokenizer from `clip_data[0][\"spiece_model\"]`), projection second. Both live in\n`models/text_encoders`, so nothing is needed in `models/checkpoints`.\n\n**2. The audio branch is not optional.** `ltx-2.3-22b-dev` is an **AV** DiT.\n`av_model.py` builds audio RoPE unconditionally, so a video-only latent gives it\nzero audio tokens and it crashes in `freqs_cis_matrix`. Every upstream 2.3\nworkflow concatenates an audio latent for exactly this reason.\n`LTXVAudioVAELoader` lists from the (empty) `checkpoints` folder, but core\n`VAELoader` reads `models/vae` -- where `LTX23_audio_vae_bf16.safetensors` is.\nThe audio half is split back off and discarded here.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
